    "case_summary": "A 22-year-old man with no significant medical history presented to the emergency department with 1 day of acute left flank pain. While hospitalized, the patient developed cyclical fevers.\n\nNo specific physical examination findings were detailed in the case report.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': '- Elevated lactate dehydrogenase- Immunostains revealed a malignant spindle cell tumor- Cytogenetics by fluorescence in situ hybridization showed SS18 (SYT;18q11.2) translocation- Immunohistochemistry positive for epithelial membrane antigen (EMA)', 'Imaging Studies': '- Initial computed tomography (CT) showed renal infarction- Trans-thoracic echocardiography (TTE) showed a large left ventricular (LV) mass- Trans-esophageal echocardiography (TEE) noted a pedunculated LV mass emanating from the basal septum protruding into the LV cavity- Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging (cMRI) confirmed the mass locationImage Descriptions:Figure 1: Renal infarction on computed tomographyFigure 2: Left ventricular mass on cardiac magnetic resonance imagingFigure 3: Hematoxylin & eosin staining of cardiac tissue biopsy at 200x magnification', 'Pathology': 'Endomyocardial biopsies revealed a malignant spindle cell tumor, histology consistent with the monophasic variant'}",
